`ready` answers "what should I do next", and until `A1-125` it answered in id
order: `A1-020` above `A1-113`, because twenty sorts before a hundred and
thirteen. A list presented as an answer, in an order that means nothing, teaches
a reader that the order means nothing, and then a real order is not believed
either.

The order is **derived** (ADR-0017), so it carries no claim the tool could not
check. Here it disagrees with id order at every position.

```
$ aplan ready
Z-9  Unblocks the one behind it (1d)
B-1  The long chain's second half (5d)
C-1  Off the spine, three days (3d)
C-2  Off the spine, three days as well (3d)
D-1  Unestimated, so nothing ranks it

```

`--explain` says why, because an order nobody can interrogate is one people learn
to distrust. `Z-9` is first for the only reason that is about the *plan* rather
than the task: finishing it makes `A-1` workable. `B-1` is next because it is on
the critical path, where slack is zero by construction. `C-1` and `C-2` are
ordered by effort, largest first: a cut cannot finish sooner than its largest
remaining task. `D-1` says outright that nothing ranks it, rather than sitting
somewhere unexplained.

```
$ aplan ready --explain
Z-9  Unblocks the one behind it (1d)
    releases 1, a chain of 1 behind it, 1d
B-1  The long chain's second half (5d)
    on the critical path, 5d
C-1  Off the spine, three days (3d)
    3d
C-2  Off the spine, three days as well (3d)
    3d
D-1  Unestimated, so nothing ranks it
    nothing to rank it by; ordered by id

```

The rank is the same answer the machine surface gives, so a rollup asking for
"the next three" cannot derive a different order than the command.

```
$ aplan tasks --format json
...
      "id": "Z-9",
...
      "ready": true,
      "milestone_task": false,
      "rank": 1,
...
```

`tree` answers "what is in my way", and most of what it printed was work already
finished. A `done` task cannot block anything, which is exactly what `ready`
computes, so `--live` shows the part that can still block and counts the rest
(`A1-217`). The default is unchanged; `A1-235` announces the runway for moving
it.

```
$ aplan tree B-1
B-1  todo  The long chain's second half
  rank: on the critical path, 5d
  waits on:
    B-0  done  The long chain's first half
  releases: nothing

```

```
$ aplan tree B-1 --live
B-1  todo  The long chain's second half
  rank: on the critical path, 5d
  waits on:
    ... 1 satisfied task not shown, past --live
  releases: nothing

```

The view says what it withheld, which is the same instinct `--depth` already had.
